them receive citations. And the biggest mistake&nbsp; that holds them back is that they blindly follow&nbsp;&nbsp; the rulebooks of academic writing style. But the real tragedy is not that they don’t&nbsp;&nbsp; get citations; it’s that their research doesn’t&nbsp; impact all the lives that it was destined to.&nbsp; Imagine if Watson &amp; Crick’s discovery of the&nbsp; structure of DNA had never been published;&nbsp;&nbsp; there would be less understanding of genetic&nbsp; disease, no CRISPR, and no personalized medicine.&nbsp; Fortunately, Watson and Crick were good&nbsp; writers, and their use of academic style&nbsp;&nbsp; is one of the reasons why their discovery had&nbsp; such a great impact on the scientific community.&nbsp; So today we’re going to follow in their&nbsp; footsteps by looking at three tips for academic&nbsp;&nbsp; writing style that have been scientifically&nbsp; proven to increase the likelihood that your&nbsp;&nbsp; research will get published in high impact&nbsp; journals and cited by others in your field. Welcome back to Writer Science, the channel&nbsp; where we explore the art and science of&nbsp;&nbsp; professional and academic writing.If you’re&nbsp; new here, my name is William and I’m an English&nbsp;&nbsp; professor based in South Korea, and for the&nbsp; writing tips in this video come from an analysis&nbsp;&nbsp; of over 30,000 articles with high impact ratings&nbsp; – that is, those published in top journals or&nbsp;&nbsp; which received a ton of citations. And the results&nbsp; might surprise you – they certainly surprised me. Now of course, scientific ideas spread because&nbsp; they are valuable. But let me ask you this:&nbsp;&nbsp; If a tree falls in the forest and&nbsp; nobody hears it, does it make a sound?&nbsp; And if you make a discovery and nobody&nbsp; reads about it, does it make an impact?&nbsp; The main reason why so many academic researchers&nbsp; don’t get the attention they deserve is not&nbsp;&nbsp; because their work isn’t valuable; it’s&nbsp; because of the biggest mistake going:&nbsp;&nbsp; they just blindly follow the academic writing&nbsp; rulebooks and never learn how to use academic&nbsp;&nbsp; writing style effectively, and for that&nbsp; reason their ideas are dead in the water. Luckily, the first common mistake&nbsp; to avoid is pretty straightforward: In the propagation of ideas, it’s&nbsp; essential to prioritize the cognitive&nbsp;&nbsp; accessibility of the discourse over the&nbsp; display of the originator's erudition.&nbsp; Got it? Good. Moving on. The second mistake is… According to the rule books, academic writing&nbsp; is supposed to be complex. If it’s too simple,&nbsp;&nbsp; they say, then it will make the ideas&nbsp; seem simple and unimportant, and they’ll&nbsp;&nbsp; never get published in high-impact journals. And it’s actually true that the complexity of&nbsp;&nbsp; your writing has a big effect on whether the&nbsp; research will be accepted for publication,&nbsp;&nbsp; except that it’s in exactly&nbsp; the opposite direction. This study by Oppenheimer took a group of&nbsp; original essays, and then kept the ideas&nbsp;&nbsp; the same but increased the complexity of&nbsp; the language. If the rule books are right,&nbsp;&nbsp; then you would expect that the more complex&nbsp; articles would have higher acceptance rates&nbsp;&nbsp; than the simpler originals. But exactly&nbsp; the opposite is true. The essays with the&nbsp;&nbsp; highest complexity were rated worse than&nbsp; the moderately complex ones, which in turn&nbsp;&nbsp; were rated worse than the simple originals. And this is true across genres. From college&nbsp;&nbsp; admissions essays to research reports, simpler&nbsp; language leads to higher acceptance rates. And to show you why that is, I need&nbsp; you to consider the way ideas spread. If an idea is carried by complex language,&nbsp; then it will never make it off the page&nbsp;&nbsp; and into the mind of the reader. That’s&nbsp; because there’s only so much work that&nbsp;&nbsp; a reader is willing to do to unpack&nbsp; your writing and find your message. On the other hand, when an idea is presented&nbsp; in clear language, it can move easily from&nbsp;&nbsp; the page to the mind of our reader. And&nbsp; when the reader can grasp our idea easily,&nbsp;&nbsp; they’ll be more likely to carry it and pass&nbsp; it on to others in the form of citation.&nbsp; Many academic writers like to dress up their ideas&nbsp; in complex language in order to make themselves&nbsp;&nbsp; appear smart, but this just turns off the reader.&nbsp; So the goal of our writing should not be to make&nbsp;&nbsp; ourselves seem smart; instead, we should write&nbsp; in a way that makes the reader feel smart.&nbsp; Now one thing I have to make clear here&nbsp; is that we’re not talking about dumbing&nbsp;&nbsp; down our ideas. We don’t want to talk to&nbsp; the reader like a fifth grader. What we’re&nbsp;&nbsp; talking about is packaging our ideas in a way&nbsp; that will be easier for the reader to process.&nbsp; But how do we reduce complexity? This is our idea; and this is the&nbsp;&nbsp; DNA of the idea. Except we don't&nbsp; call it DNA; we call it Syntax. Syntax is like the DNA of an idea because&nbsp; it’s code we use to share and replicate&nbsp;&nbsp; ideas. If the code is complex, then&nbsp; there are going to be mutations in&nbsp;&nbsp; the DNA and the idea won’t make&nbsp; it into the mind of the reader.&nbsp;&nbsp; So we need to make sure the code is as&nbsp; simple and straightforward as possible. Here's the same idea presented&nbsp; in two different codes. Which one is easier to understand? It's the second one, of course, because in&nbsp; the DNA of ideas, there are only two elements:&nbsp;&nbsp; content words (like nouns, verbs, and adjectives);&nbsp;&nbsp; and function words (like&nbsp; prepositions and determiners).&nbsp; Each of these codes use the same content words;&nbsp; but one uses way more function words than the&nbsp;&nbsp; other. When a sentence has a lot of these function&nbsp; words, it becomes heavy with all these branches. Academic articles written with fewer articles&nbsp;&nbsp; and fewer prepositions in the&nbsp; introduction get more citations.&nbsp; The fewer function words, the simpler the&nbsp; sentence; and the simpler the sentence,&nbsp;&nbsp; the easier it is for the reader to get our idea&nbsp; and the more likely they will be to share that&nbsp;&nbsp; idea with others, which brings us to the&nbsp; second rule about academic writing style. According to the rule books, academic writing&nbsp; should be formal and impersonal. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp; we’re not talking about literature here;&nbsp;&nbsp; this is science, and science is objective.&nbsp; This objectivity, according to the rule book,&nbsp;&nbsp; should be reflected in the writing style&nbsp; itself. It doesn’t matter who does the research;&nbsp;&nbsp; what matters is the research itself. That’s why so many academic writers&nbsp;&nbsp; choose to use passive sentences and avoid&nbsp; the use of personal pronouns. Pssst... and&nbsp;&nbsp; it’s also why their research fails to&nbsp; pick up publications and citations. To understand why impersonal language doesn’t&nbsp; work in academic writing, take a look at this:&nbsp; It's a Klein bottle, and it represents a&nbsp; mathematical surface that has no edges or&nbsp;&nbsp; orientation - no outside, and no&nbsp; inside. It can be thought of as a&nbsp;&nbsp; higher-dimension analogue to the Mobius&nbsp; strip, but, unlike the Mobius strip,&nbsp;&nbsp; which has only one side and one boundary,&nbsp; the Klein bottle has no boundaries at all.&nbsp; Now compare that description of the Klein&nbsp; bottle with this one, from a popular TED talk.&nbsp; Which presentation are you&nbsp; more likely to remember?&nbsp; The information is the same, but when it’s&nbsp; presented by a researcher who is clearly&nbsp;&nbsp; passionate about the Klein bottle, it’s a&nbsp; lot easier for us to share that interest. People relate to other people, and&nbsp; readers are people, so when there’s&nbsp;&nbsp; a person present in the story, it makes&nbsp; it a lot easier for the reader to connect&nbsp;&nbsp; to the story and receive the ideas.&nbsp; And that connection makes it a lot&nbsp;&nbsp; easier for the writer to persuade the&nbsp; reader that the research is valuable. Now it’s important to note that this doesn't work&nbsp; in every place throughout the entire article.&nbsp; Personal language only helps&nbsp; with citations when they’re in&nbsp;&nbsp; the front matter, which is the&nbsp; introduction and the abstract. By contrast, articles that use personal&nbsp; language in the middle section–the methods&nbsp;&nbsp; and results–actually receive fewer citations. And to understand why that is, we need to&nbsp;&nbsp; consider the purpose of these sections. The purpose of the mid section is to unpack&nbsp;&nbsp; the finer details of the experiment itself.&nbsp; It’s sometimes known as the empirical section. At this point of the report, the reader&nbsp; doesn’t care about the story; instead,&nbsp;&nbsp; readers come to this section to examine&nbsp; the author’s methods and findings.&nbsp; So it makes sense that the most successful authors&nbsp; would use passive and impersonal sentences,&nbsp;&nbsp; which hides the presence of the&nbsp; author and highlight the work itself.&nbsp; On the other hand, the purpose of&nbsp; the front matter–the abstract and&nbsp;&nbsp; introduction–is to motivate our reader,&nbsp; to get them interested in our research. If we introduce this research using&nbsp; impersonal language, then it’s like the&nbsp;&nbsp; Klein bottle; the reader’s not going to care. But like the mad scientist in the TED Talk,&nbsp;&nbsp; the most successful articles use personal&nbsp; language in the abstract and introduction&nbsp;&nbsp; in order to capture the reader’s interest and&nbsp; motivate them to read the rest of the report.&nbsp; But not only can personal language&nbsp; help capture the reader’s interest,&nbsp;&nbsp; it also changes the way the reader perceives you. When you take personal ownership of ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp; it makes you seem more authoritative. If you&nbsp; introduce valuable research with personal&nbsp;&nbsp; language, readers will associate that value with&nbsp; you, making you seem more credible and important.&nbsp; So if your work is any good, then don’t be&nbsp; shy; let the reader know that it’s yours,&nbsp;&nbsp; especially in the front matter. If you follow the lousy rule books&nbsp;&nbsp; and stick with impersonal language, your&nbsp; research will appear lifeless and detached,&nbsp;&nbsp; as though the ideas–your ideas–are&nbsp; just out there floating in the ether. But to understand the third and final tip&nbsp; about academic writing style, I want you&nbsp;&nbsp; to take a look at these two restaurant&nbsp; reviews. Which one seems more appetizing? Many people would agree that the one written&nbsp; in the present tense sounds more convincing. But if the present tense is more convincing,&nbsp;&nbsp; then why do so many academic rule books tell us&nbsp; to write about research using the past tense? A lot of people think that they have to use&nbsp; the past tense to talk about research in the&nbsp;&nbsp; past. Makes sense, right? It's the past tense;&nbsp; research happened in the past. But that's wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp; It's kind of a deeper grammatical point that&nbsp; we don't have to get into here, but the basic&nbsp;&nbsp; message is that tense has nothing to do with&nbsp; time, and there's no rule that says you have&nbsp;&nbsp; to use the past tense to talk about research&nbsp; just because it happened in the past. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp; there are some really good reasons to use&nbsp; the present tense whenever you talk about&nbsp;&nbsp; your own research. When we divorce tense&nbsp; from time, it becomes really easy to see&nbsp;&nbsp; why authors who write about their research&nbsp; in the present tense receive more citations.&nbsp; Just like the restaurant reviews, the present&nbsp; tense makes research seem more current, relevant,&nbsp;&nbsp; and applicable. The past tense, sounds like&nbsp; old news; it's passé, stale, and forgotten. So when writing about your own research,&nbsp; using the present tense will make it seem&nbsp;&nbsp; more current, more alive, which makes it&nbsp; more likely to get picked up for citation.&nbsp; Yes, the research happened in the past; but if we&nbsp; follow the rulebooks and write about our research&nbsp;&nbsp; using the so-called past tense, then&nbsp; it’s going to seem like old news,&nbsp;&nbsp; so breath life into your research&nbsp; by using the present tense. So I really hope you see that, if your research&nbsp; isn't getting the attention it deserves,&nbsp;&nbsp; it’s really not your fault; it’s the&nbsp; fault of the old academic rule books.&nbsp;&nbsp; But to tell you the truth, if it's&nbsp; citations that you’re looking for,&nbsp;&nbsp; you would actually be better off not writing&nbsp; a research report at all and instead,&nbsp;&nbsp; you need to check out this video right here&nbsp; because it’s going to show you how to write&nbsp;&nbsp; a different kind of academic article that has&nbsp; been shown to get cited way more often and over&nbsp;&nbsp; longer periods of time than the standard IMRaD&nbsp; research report, so be sure to go check it out.


